nt into the environment world).',\n 'The track system and car: blocks with exact port frames, a ribbon mesher for render and collision geometry, our own Rapier query world, a surface-following car that holds loops at speed and drops off when slow, and the autopilot that drives every track (tests, medals, rivals, attract).',\n \"Getting stunt racing (loops, banks, jumps) onto an engine whose vehicle is a rolling ball: none of world.vehicle or world.track could do loops, so the road, physics queries, camera up vector and ghost rendering all had to be built or patched.\",\n 'E1 pen.frame(f, r, u) for any orientation; E2 a camera pose with its own up vector; E3 translucent ghost batches; E4 analog trigger values (input.analog); E5 PlayStation glyphs by default with Xbox as the option; E6 exported TOON_BANDS and a lambert helper. All listed in engine/CHANGES.md.',\n 'Make split-screen overlays viewport-relative (or document it), and add a road/track primitive that supports arbitrary up vectors (loops and banks) with ray queries, so racing games do not need their own physics world.'\n]\nd['promptFeedback']=[\n 'Exact deliverables (file names, the exit-interview shape, the four report sections) and a detailed plan that had already de-risked the hard parts (bundling, the car model, the AI Hub protocol), plus clear rules for sharing the machine.',\n 'The plan and TASK.md disagree on names (the plan was written for another run: max effort, another date); TASK.md wins, but it took a moment to be sure. \"Usage\" asks for cost and tokens the model cannot see.',\n 'Scope: a TrackMania-style game with race, puzzle, survival, a track editor, hot seat, split screen, AI Hub online, replays, a garage, Dev Mode, attract mode and a 10-foot UI, all in one session and one file.',\n 'Rank the features (must / should / could) so a single session can make deliberate cuts, and give the agent its token and time usage so the Usage field can be answered.'\n]\njson.dump(d, open(p,'w'), indent=2, 